{% partial file="./_breaking-changes-caution.mdoc" /%}

## Getting Started

Use the first-party [Cookie Consent module](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/modules/tree/448e4c368b6b531a254b60901bf6fb447ce7f4c0/Cookieconsent) as a reference for the manifest, admin controller, and Twig templates.

{% steps %}
1. Create `modules/Example/` in a development installation, or `src/modules/Example/` in a source checkout. Use `example` as the manifest ID and `Box\Mod\Example` as the PHP namespace. The folder name is the lowercase ID with its first letter capitalized. Start product-type module names with `Service`, as in `Servicehosting`.
2. Adapt the package's `manifest.json` for your module. Put your module's logic in `Service.php`. If you need an admin page, use `Controller/Admin.php` to register its route and put the Twig template in `templates/admin/`.
3. In the admin panel, go to **Extensions → Overview** and select **Activate** next to your module. If you added an admin page, open it to check that the route and template load.
{% /steps %}

For template-specific helpers, see [Twig Filters & Functions](/extensions-and-development/twig-filters/). For browser-side API forms and links, see the [JavaScript API Wrapper](/extensions-and-development/javascript/).

## The Manifest

Set `manifest_version` to `1` and `type` to `"module"`. Use `requires` to declare supported FOSSBilling and PHP versions and any module dependencies. See the [manifest schema](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/modules/blob/main/manifest.schema.json) for all fields and validation rules.

You can place an icon next to `manifest.json`. See the [module file structure](/extensions-and-development/file-structure/#srcmodules) for supported icon formats and other entry points.

## Running Scheduled Tasks

Use `onBeforeAdminCronRun` or `onAfterAdminCronRun` for scheduled work. Define the handler in `Service.php` and adapt the event-specific method name and body in the [Event Hooks example](/extensions-and-development/event-hooks/#using-hooks-in-your-module).

## Module Permissions

Define custom permissions for staff members in your module.

### Setting Up Permissions

```php
public function getModulePermissions(): array
{
    return [
        'delete_something' => [
            'type' => 'bool',
            'display_name' => __trans('Delete something'),
            'description' => __trans('Allows the staff member to delete "something"'),
        ],
        'can_always_access' => true,
        'manage_settings' => [],
    ];
}
```

`can_always_access` grants baseline access to the module. Declaring `manage_settings` registers the standard boolean permission for editing the module's settings.

### Checking Permissions

In PHP handlers, use `checkPermissionsAndThrowException()` to enforce a permission for the current staff member:

```php
$this->di['mod_service']('Staff')->checkPermissionsAndThrowException('example', 'delete_something');
```

In admin Twig templates, use `has_permission()` to show controls for that permission:

```twig {% process=false %}
{% if has_permission('example', 'delete_something') %}
    <button type="button">{{ 'Delete something'|trans }}</button>
{% endif %}
```

Pass just the module ID, as in `has_permission('example')`, to check module access. Enforce permissions in the PHP handler even if Twig hides the control.
